\section{Verification}{

\code{verify} decides what a step does with \code{check_by} variables once it has
a single candidate:
\itemize{
\item \code{"none"}: accept it.
\item \code{"no_conflict"}: reject it if any check variable is present on both
sides and disagrees.
\item \code{"agree"}: accept it only if at least one check variable is present on
both sides and agrees.
}

Without \code{check_by} variables every mode accepts. The defaults are
\code{"none"} for the stricter steps (exact, typo, nickname) and
\code{"no_conflict"} for the looser ones (tokens, Soundex, first word, initial,
compound last name).
}

\section{Warning}{

\code{match_soundex()} confuses relatives at the same address. Family members
share a last name and a zip code, and many different first names share a
Soundex code (MICHELLE / MICHAEL, DON / DEANNA, RUSSELL / RACHEL), so a zip
check cannot catch these false matches. On the 2022 example file roughly
20 of 75 Soundex matches that agreed on zip were wrong. Review its matches
by hand (\code{nm_results(m)} filtered to \code{match_step == "soundex"}) before
using them.
}

